      "final_content": "**Bug vs. Feature – What the system shows**\n\n| Project | Bug (example) | Feature (example) |\n|---------|---------------|-------------------|\n| **ATLAS** | *Crash on large upload* (ATLAS‑106) – a defect that should not happen. | *Improve onboarding copy* (ATLAS‑107) – a requested improvement to the user experience. |\n|         | Title is about a problem, body explains the failure, priority is **high/urgent**, and it carries labels like `bug` or `regression`. | Title is about a desired change, body explains the request, priority is **low**, and it carries labels like `feature` or `good‑first‑issue`. |\n\n**Key differences observed in the system**\n\n1. **Intent**  \n   *Bug*: Intended to be fixed.  \n   *Feature*: Intended to be added or enhanced.\n\n2. **Priority**  \n   *Bug*: Usually **high** or **urgent** because it blocks users.  \n   *Feature*: Usually **low** (or sometimes medium/high if it’s a priority request).\n\n3. **Assignability**  \n   *Bug*: Often assigned to a developer who can reproduce and fix it.  \n   *Feature*: May be left unassigned or assigned to a designer/lead.\n\n4. **Labels**  \n   *Bug*: Common labels are `bug`, `regression`, `needs-repro`.  \n   *Feature*: Common labels are `feature`, `docs`, `good-first-issue`.\n\n5. **Lifecycle**  \n   *Bug*: Typically opened, worked on, and closed with a comment confirming the fix.  \n   *Feature*: Opened, discussed, and either closed (if rejected) or merged into a release.\n\n**In short:**  \nA **bug** is a problem that needs fixing; a **feature** is a requested improvement that adds new functionality. The system treats them with different priorities, labels, and typical assignees, reflecting their distinct roles in the product lifecycle.",
